  "body": "Last of four from the fallback audit.\n\n## 1. `error_kind` spoke two languages\n\nAn `ApplicationError` carries a `FailureKind` as a class attribute — but only `ExecutionFailure` was ever read. So the request log recorded `timeout` and `rate_limit` alongside `InvalidRequestError` and `ApplicationUnavailableError`: the same failure spelled two ways, splitting any grouping by that column. On 21 days of live traffic that was **102 of 1,081 errors**.\n\n`failure_kind()` / `failure_kind_name()` now read all three shapes in `core/failures.py`, and both the request row and the attempt log use them. The private copy added in #165 is gone.\n\n## 2. A malformed request walked the whole chain\n\nA 400 is the caller's, not the model's — the same body fails identically everywhere, so a three-model chain bought three round trips to the same error and three log rows all saying the request was invalid.\n\n`FALLBACK_SKIP_KINDS` lists the kinds that end a route instead of moving on. Default `invalid_request`; **empty means fall back on absolutely everything**, which is the literal reading of what you asked for and is one setting away.\n\n**A deny-list, not an allow-list, on purpose.** A failure kind added later falls back by default — the safe direction. An allow-list would silently stop covering it.\n\nThe skipped models say *why*:\n\n```\n0  failed    groq/llama-3.3-70b     invalid_request  messages: field required\n1  not tried openrouter/glm-5       route_ended      not tried: a invalid_request\n                                                     failure ends the route\n```\n\nThat's the difference between \"your chain did not help\" and \"your chain was correctly not used\" — legible only because of #165.\n\n**An unknown kind is refused at startup**, not ignored. A typo would fail *open*: the name never matches, the route falls back on everything, and the setting looks applied while doing nothing.\n\n## Architecture note\n\n`config` is a leaf package by declared policy — it imports nothing, not even `core` — so settings validation cannot reach `FailureKind`. My first attempt imported it anyway and `test_package_dependencies_follow_declarative_policy` caught it. The names are mirrored in `config/constants.py` and **pinned equal in both directions** by a contract test, because a list that mirrors another file drifts and only one of the two drift directions is loud.\n\nThe field went to the **routing** section rather than Limits: every Limits field must be a bounded numeric (an existing guard caught the misplacement), and it belongs beside the chains it governs anyway.\n\n## Verification\n\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing **by name**:\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n| --- | --- |\n| M1 skip list ignored, a 400 walks the chain | `malformed_request_does_not_walk_the_whole_chain` |\n| M2 every failure ends the route | 13 tests, including every pre-existing fallback contract |\n| M3 `ApplicationError` kind ignored | `application_error_is_named_by_its_kind` |\n| M4 config mirror drifts from `FailureKind` | `config_mirrors_the_failure_kinds_it_cannot_import` |\n\nSettings **probed with real values** rather than read: a good list normalises (`\" invalid_request , timeout \"` → `invalid_request,timeout`), empty is accepted and means everything, and `nonsense` is refused at load with the known kinds listed.\n\nLocal CI: `ruff format --check`, `ruff check`, `ty` clean; pytest **4,823 passed** with the documented 6 `test_install_ps1_*` baseline failures.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **Nothing backfills old rows.** `error_kind` stays as recorded for requests that predate this, so grouping across the boundary still needs both spellings.\n- **A 400 from a gateway that misclassifies** would now end the route where it previously moved on. If a provider returns 400 for something a different model could serve, `FALLBACK_SKIP_KINDS=` (empty) restores the old behaviour.\n- Not exercised end-to-end against a live upstream that returns a genuine 400 — covered by unit tests with a provider double.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
