    "message": "feat(routing): one failure vocabulary, and a configurable fallback trigger\n\nLast of four from the fallback audit.\n\n1. `error_kind` spoke two languages\n-----------------------------------\nAn `ApplicationError` carries a `FailureKind` as a class attribute, but only\n`ExecutionFailure` was ever read, so the request log recorded `timeout` and\n`rate_limit` alongside `InvalidRequestError` and `ApplicationUnavailableError`\n-- the same failure spelled two ways, splitting any grouping by that column.\nOn 21 days of the author's traffic that was 102 of 1,081 errors.\n\n`failure_kind()` and `failure_kind_name()` now read all three shapes in\n`core/failures.py`, and both the request row and the attempt log use them.\nThe per-attempt helper added in #165 was a private copy of this; it is gone.\n\n2. A malformed request walked the whole chain\n---------------------------------------------\nA 400 is the caller's, not the model's: the same body fails identically\neverywhere, so a three-model chain bought three round trips to the same error\nand three log rows all saying the request was invalid.\n\nFALLBACK_SKIP_KINDS lists the kinds that end a route instead of moving on.\nDefault `invalid_request`; empty means fall back on absolutely everything.\n\nA deny-list, not an allow-list, on purpose: a failure kind added later falls\nback by default, which is the safe direction. An allow-list would silently\nstop covering it.\n\nThe skipped models say why. `not tried: a invalid_request failure ends the\nroute` is the difference between \"your chain did not help\" and \"your chain was\ncorrectly not used\" -- which is only legible because of #165.\n\nAn unknown kind is refused at startup rather than ignored. A typo would fail\nopen: the name never matches, the route falls back on everything, and the\nsetting looks applied while doing nothing.\n\nArchitecture\n------------\n`config` is a leaf package by declared policy -- it imports nothing, not even\n`core` -- so settings validation cannot reach `FailureKind`. The names are\nmirrored in `config/constants.py` and pinned equal in both directions by a\ncontract test, because a list that mirrors another file drifts and only one\nof the two drift directions is loud.\n\nThe field went to the routing section rather than Limits: every field in\nLimits must be a bounded numeric, enforced by an existing guard that caught\nthe misplacement, and the setting belongs beside the chains it governs anyway.\n\nVerification\n------------\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing by name:\n\n    M1 skip list ignored, a 400 walks the chain  -> malformed_request_does_not_walk\n    M2 every failure ends the route              -> 13 tests, incl. every existing\n                                                    fallback contract\n    M3 ApplicationError kind ignored             -> application_error_named_by_kind\n    M4 config mirror drifts from FailureKind     -> config_mirrors_the_failure_kinds\n\nSettings probed with real values rather than read: a good list normalises\n(`\" invalid_request , timeout \"` -> `invalid_request,timeout`), empty is\naccepted and means everything, and `nonsense` is refused at load with the\nknown kinds listed.\n\nLocal CI: ruff format --check, ruff check, ty clean; pytest 4,823 passed with\nthe documented 6 test_install_ps1_* baseline failures.\n\nNot done\n--------\nNothing backfills the old rows: `error_kind` stays as recorded for requests\nthat predate this. Grouping across the boundary still needs both spellings.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
